   "text": {
    "L1": "When the Mauryan empire ended around 185 BCE, power did not simply disappear.",
    "L2": "It was reorganised among many regional kingdoms, including the Shungas in Magadha.",
    "L3": "The Kushanas, Indo-Greeks and Shakas ruled in the north and north-west, while the Satavahanas ruled in the Deccan.",
    "L4": "Trade grew by land and sea, linking India with Central Asia, China, the Roman world and Southeast Asia.",
    "L5": "Ports such as Muziris, Arikamedu and Bharuch exported pepper, spices, cotton cloth and gems.",
    "L6": "Coins and inscriptions give evidence about rulers and connections. Roman coins in south India show trade, not Roman rule.",
    "L7": "Art and literature flourished through Gandhara, Mathura and Tamil Sangam traditions, while ideas travelled with merchants and monks.",
    "L8": "That is why this period is an age of reorganisation, not an age when nothing important happened."
   },
